@page "/docs/components/tab"

<Seo Canonical="/docs/components/tab" Title="Blazorise Tab component" Description="Organize related content into tabbed panels with the Blazorise Tabs component. Learn how to connect tabs to panels and support accessible keyboard navigation." />

<DocsPageTitle Path="Components/Tab">
    Blazorise Tab component
</DocsPageTitle>

<DocsPageLead>
    Tabs organize related content into panels, allowing users to switch between them without leaving the page.
</DocsPageLead>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Use tabs for related sections that do not need to appear together, such as account settings.
    The <Code Tag>Tabs</Code> component manages selection and shows one panel at a time:
</DocsPageParagraph>

<UnorderedList>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Paragraph>
            <Code Tag>Items</Code> contains the <Code Tag>Tab</Code> components that users select to switch panels.
        </Paragraph>
    </UnorderedListItem>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Paragraph>
            <Code Tag>Content</Code> contains the <Code Tag>TabPanel</Code> components that hold each tab's content.
        </Paragraph>
    </UnorderedListItem>
</UnorderedList>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Give each <Code>Tab</Code> and its <Code>TabPanel</Code> the same <Code>Name</Code>. Use <Code>SelectedTab</Code>
    to set the initial selection, or bind it to keep selection in sync with your application state.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Usually, tabs and panels belong together inside <Code>Tabs</Code>. To render panels elsewhere, use
    <Code Tag>TabsContent</Code> and bind its <Code>SelectedPanel</Code> to the same value as <Code>Tabs.SelectedTab</Code>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Examples
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Basic">
        Match each tab and panel by <Code>Name</Code>. This example starts on Profile and updates
        <Code>SelectedTab</Code> through <Code>SelectedTabChanged</Code> when another tab is selected.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="BasicTabExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <BasicTabExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Lazy Load">
        <Code>TabsRenderMode.LazyLoad</Code> creates each panel's content on its first visit and keeps it afterward.
        Enter values below and switch tabs to see how the inputs retain their state.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="LazyLoadTabExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <LazyLoadTabExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSection>
    <DocsPageSectionHeader Title="Lazy Reload">
        <Code>TabsRenderMode.LazyReload</Code> renders only the active panel's content and recreates it on each visit.
        These inputs keep their values locally, so switching tabs clears them. Bind values to parent state when they need to persist.
    </DocsPageSectionHeader>
    <DocsPageSectionContent Code="LazyReloadTabExample" Outlined FullWidth>
        <LazyReloadTabExample />
    </DocsPageSectionContent>
</DocsPageSection>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Accessibility
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Naming the Group
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Screen readers can announce a name for the whole group. For example, set <Code>AriaLabel="Account settings"</Code>
    on <Code>Tabs</Code>. To use an existing visible heading instead, set <Code>AriaLabelledBy</Code> to that heading's ID.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Keyboard Navigation
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Press <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> to reach the selected tab, then use the left and right arrow keys to move between tabs.
    Vertical tabs use the up and down arrow keys instead. Disabled tabs are skipped. <Kbd>Home</Kbd> and <Kbd>End</Kbd>
    move to the first and last available tabs. Press <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> or <Kbd>Space</Kbd> to open the chosen tab.
    Arrow keys alone do not change the displayed content.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Panel Focus
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    By default, pressing <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> after the selected tab moves to the open panel, where users can read or scroll its content.
    Set <Code>Focusable="false"</Code> on <Code>TabPanel</Code> to skip the panel itself.
    Buttons, links, and form controls inside it remain reachable with <Kbd>Tab</Kbd>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Best Practices
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Group Peer Content
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Tabs are suited to a small set of peer sections within the same context, not sequential steps or unrelated destinations. Keep labels short, distinct, and ordered according to user needs. Information required to understand or complete the current task should not be hidden only in an inactive tab.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Loading Behavior
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Preserve panel state when users are likely to switch between tabs while comparing or entering information. Reload content only when freshness is more important than continuity, and make any resulting loading state clear.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Clear Labels
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Use short, descriptive labels so users know what each tab contains.
    Give the group a name that explains its purpose to screen reader users.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSubsectionTitle IncludeInToc="false">
    Keyboard Access
</DocsPageSubsectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Keep the focus highlight visible so users can see which tab they are about to open.
    Keep panels reachable by keyboard when they contain introductory text or need scrolling.
    Skip the panel itself only when users can go straight to its controls without missing useful content.
</DocsPageParagraph>

